Feature: Compound Drug Screening Analysis and Categorization

  Background:
    Given the source table "purgo_playground.compound_drug_analysis" exists in Unity Catalog schema "purgo_playground"
    And the table contains columns: therapeutic_area, ic50, auc, efficacy, sample_size, approved_flag, validation_status, score1, score2, score3, score4, score5

  Scenario: Filter and aggregate compound analysis data by therapeutic area
    Given the dataset is loaded from "purgo_playground.compound_drug_analysis"
    When the data is filtered to include only rows where approved_flag = '1' and validation_status = 'valid'
    And the filtered data is grouped by therapeutic_area
    Then for each therapeutic_area, calculate:
      | metric            | calculation         |
      | avg_ic50          | average(ic50)       |
      | avg_auc           | average(auc)        |
      | avg_efficacy      | average(efficacy)   |
      | total_sample_size | sum(sample_size)    |
      | study_count       | count(*)            |

  Scenario: Join filtered data with aggregated metrics
    Given the filtered data and aggregated metrics per therapeutic_area
    When the filtered data is joined with aggregated metrics on therapeutic_area using an inner join
    Then each row contains all original columns plus the aggregated metrics

  Scenario Outline: Compute overall_score and categorize potential
    Given a row with score1=<score1>, score2=<score2>, score3=<score3>, score4=<score4>, score5=<score5>
    When overall_score is calculated as average of score1, score2, score3, score4, score5
    Then the potential category is assigned as follows:
      | overall_score | potential_category   |
      | 70 <= score <= 100 | High Potential      |
      | 60 <= score < 70   | Moderate Potential  |
      | score < 60         | Low Potential       |
    Examples:
      | score1 | score2 | score3 | score4 | score5 | expected_overall_score | expected_category    |
      | 80     | 90     | 85     | 75     | 80     | 82                    | High Potential       |
      | 65     | 62     | 68     | 66     | 64     | 65                    | Moderate Potential   |
      | 55     | 58     | 59     | 57     | 56     | 57                    | Low Potential        |
      | 100    | 100    | 100    | 100    | 100    | 100                   | High Potential       |
      | 70     | 70     | 70     | 70     | 70     | 70                    | High Potential       |
      | 60     | 60     | 60     | 60     | 60     | 60                    | Moderate Potential   |
      | 59     | 59     | 59     | 59     | 59     | 59                    | Low Potential        |

  Scenario: Final output includes all columns and result analysis
    Given the joined data with all original and aggregated columns
    And each row has computed overall_score and potential_category
    When the final output is generated
    Then the output includes:
      | Column                |
      | therapeutic_area      |
      | ic50                  |
      | auc                   |
      | efficacy              |
      | sample_size           |
      | approved_flag         |
      | validation_status     |
      | score1                |
      | score2                |
      | score3                |
      | score4                |
      | score5                |
      | avg_ic50              |
      | avg_auc               |
      | avg_efficacy          |
      | total_sample_size     |
      | study_count           |
      | overall_score         |
      | potential_category    |

  Scenario Outline: Error handling for missing or invalid fields
    Given a row with <field> missing or invalid
    When the analysis is performed
    Then an error is raised with message "<error_message>"
    Examples:
      | field            | error_message                                 |
      | therapeutic_area | "Missing required field: therapeutic_area"    |
      | ic50             | "Missing or non-numeric value in ic50"        |
      | auc              | "Missing or non-numeric value in auc"         |
      | efficacy         | "Missing or non-numeric value in efficacy"    |
      | sample_size      | "Missing or non-numeric value in sample_size" |
      | score1           | "Missing or non-numeric value in score1"      |
      | score2           | "Missing or non-numeric value in score2"      |
      | score3           | "Missing or non-numeric value in score3"      |
      | score4           | "Missing or non-numeric value in score4"      |
      | score5           | "Missing or non-numeric value in score5"      |

  Scenario Outline: Error handling for invalid filter values
    Given a row with approved_flag=<approved_flag> and validation_status=<validation_status>
    When the filter is applied
    Then the row is excluded from analysis
    Examples:
      | approved_flag | validation_status |
      | 0             | valid             |
      | 1             | invalid           |
      | 0             | invalid           |
      | null          | valid             |
      | 1             | null              |

  Scenario Outline: Handling duplicate rows
    Given multiple rows with identical values for all columns
    When the data is grouped by therapeutic_area
    Then study_count reflects the total number of rows, including duplicates
    Examples:
      | therapeutic_area | duplicate_count | expected_study_count |
      | Oncology        | 2               | 2                    |
      | Cardiology      | 3               | 3                    |

  Scenario Outline: Handling nulls in score columns
    Given a row with <score_field> as null
    When overall_score is calculated
    Then the row is excluded from result analysis
    Examples:
      | score_field |
      | score1      |
      | score2      |
      | score3      |
      | score4      |
      | score5      |

  Scenario: Type validation for numeric fields
    Given a row with non-numeric values in ic50, auc, efficacy, sample_size, score1, score2, score3, score4, or score5
    When the analysis is performed
    Then an error is raised indicating the field must be numeric

  Scenario: Join type specification
    Given the join is performed on therapeutic_area
    When a therapeutic_area exists in filtered data but not in aggregated metrics (or vice versa)
    Then only rows with matching therapeutic_area in both datasets are included (inner join)

  Scenario: Output ordering
    Given the final output is generated
    When no explicit ordering is specified
    Then the output is unordered by default

# END
